For a more complex column layout, you can use the `TextFrame` object and its `columns` property. Here's an example of how to create a text field with multiple columns:

```javascript
// Create a new text field
var myTextField = new TextFrame();
myTextField.text = "Column 1\nColumn 2\nColumn 3";

// Set the text format
myTextField.textFormat.align = "justifyAll";

// Set the column count and gutter width
myTextField.columns = 3;
myTextField.gutterWidth = 10;

// Add the text field to the page
myTextField.parent = page;
```

In this example, we create a new `TextFrame` object, set its text, and then set the `align` property to "justifyAll" to justify the text within each column. We then set the `columns` property to 3 to create three columns and the `gutterWidth` property to 10 to set the width of the gutter between columns. Finally, we add the text field to the page.

Note that this will create a fixed-width column layout. If you want a more dynamic column layout, you can use a `Table` object instead:

```javascript
// Create a new table
var myTable = new Table();
myTable.columns = 3;
myTable.gutterWidth = 10;

// Create a new text field for each column
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    var cell = myTable.cells[i];
    cell.text = "Column " + (i + 1);
    cell.textFormat.align = "justifyAll";
}

// Add the table to the page
myTable.parent = page;
```

In this example, we create a new `Table` object and set its `columns` property to 3. We then create a new text field for each column and set its text and alignment. Finally, we add the table to the page.

You can also use the `TextFrame` object with a `Table` object to create a more complex layout:

```javascript
// Create a new text frame
var myTextField = new TextFrame();
myTextField.text = "Column 1\nColumn 2\nColumn 3";

// Create a new table
var myTable = new Table();
myTable.columns = 3;
myTable.gutterWidth = 10;

// Add the text frame to the table
