```

However, if you want to run the same code on multiple named variables without having to define a function, you can use a loop with a lambda expression:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 10;
    int b = 20;
    int c = 30;

    // Define a lambda expression to perform some operation
    auto processVariable = [](auto& x) {
        std::cout << "Processing variable: " << x << std::endl;
        // Add some operation here
        x *= 2;
    };

    // Run the lambda expression on multiple variables
    { processVariable(a); }
    { processVariable(b); }
    { processVariable(c); }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the lambda expression `processVariable` takes a reference to an `auto` type, which allows it to work with variables of any type. The `{}` around each call to `processVariable` is necessary to create a new scope for each call, so that the changes to `x` don't affect the next iteration.

Alternatively, you can use a loop with a range-based for loop to iterate over a collection of variables:

```cpp
